    difficulty:'Basic', order:1, isPublished:true,
    content:`## What is a Function?\nA **function** is a named block of code that performs a specific task. Define once, call anywhere.\n\n## Types of Functions\n\n### 1. Built-in Functions\nAlready in Python — no import needed.\nExamples: \`print()\`, \`len()\`, \`input()\`, \`range()\`, \`int()\`, \`str()\`, \`max()\`, \`min()\`\n\n### 2. Functions in Modules\nNeed import first.\nExamples: \`math.sqrt()\`, \`random.randint()\`\n\n### 3. User Defined Functions\nCreated by you using \`def\` keyword.\n\n## Parameters vs Arguments\n- **Parameter** — variable in definition: \`def greet(name):\` — name is parameter\n- **Argument** — actual value when calling: \`greet("Rahul")\` — Rahul is argument\n\n## Scope of Variables\n- **Local Scope** — variable inside function, only available inside it\n- **Global Scope** — variable outside all functions, available everywhere\n- Use \`global\` keyword to modify a global variable inside a function\n\n## Default Parameters\nGive a parameter a preset value. Used when caller does not pass it.\nDefault parameters must come AFTER required ones.\n\n## Flow of Execution\n1. Program starts from top\n2. Function is called → control jumps to function body\n3. Function executes\n4. Control returns to where it was called`,
    syntax:`# Built-in function\nprint(len("Hello"))  # 5\n\n# Module function\nimport math\nprint(math.sqrt(16))  # 4.0\n\n# Simple user defined function\ndef greet(name):\n    message = "Hello, " + name + "!"\n    return message\n\nprint(greet("Rahul"))  # Hello, Rahul!\n\n# Default parameter\ndef power(base, exp=2):\n    return base ** exp\n\nprint(power(3))      # 9  (exp=2 by default)\nprint(power(3, 3))   # 27\n\n# Multiple return values\ndef min_max(numbers):\n    return min(numbers), max(numbers)\n\nlow, high = min_max([3, 1, 7, 2, 9])\nprint(low, high)  # 1 9\n\n# Global variable example\ncount = 0\ndef increment():\n    global count\n    count += 1\nincrement()\nprint(count)  # 1`,
